    <body>
      <sec id="article-28278.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Rectus sheath hematoma is an uncommon yet clinically significant cause of acute abdominal pain that is frequently associated with abdominal wall trauma, forceful muscular contraction, or therapeutic anticoagulation. Because its presentation can closely mimic a wide range of intra-abdominal conditions, it often poses a diagnostic challenge in emergency and surgical settings. This activity reviews the pathophysiology, risk factors, clinical presentation, and diagnostic evaluation of rectus sheath hematoma, with emphasis on timely recognition and appropriate use of imaging, particularly computed tomography as the diagnostic gold standard. The activity also addresses key laboratory studies, including serial hemoglobin assessment and coagulation testing, to guide clinical management. Evidence-based treatment strategies are discussed, ranging from conservative supportive care and reversal of coagulopathy to angioembolization for ongoing hemorrhage. The importance of risk stratification, early intervention, and interprofessional collaboration in improving patient outcomes and reducing avoidable complications is also highlighted.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify the causes and key risk factors of rectus sheath hematoma, including trauma, forceful abdominal contraction, and anticoagulant or antiplatelet therapy.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Apply best practices&#x000a0;when deciding&#x000a0;treatment options for rectus sheath hematoma.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement an evidence-based diagnostic approach to&#x000a0;rectus sheath hematoma.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Coordinate interprofessional management among emergency clinicians, radiologists, internists, pharmacists, surgeons, and nursing staff to improve early diagnosis, reduce unnecessary invasive procedures, and optimize patient outcomes.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=28278&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=28278">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28278.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Rectus sheath hematoma is an uncommon but clinically important condition caused by bleeding within the rectus abdominis due to injury to the superior or inferior epigastric artery, most often associated with abdominal wall trauma or forceful contractions of the abdomen, especially in individuals receiving anticoagulant therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28278.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;Diagnosis of&#x000a0;rectus sheath hematoma can be difficult&#x000a0;because the presentation, a palpable mass and abdominal pain,&#x000a0;mimics a wide range of intra-abdominal pathologies. Rectus sheath hematoma represents an uncommon but important cause of acute abdominal pain, and its rarity and protean presentation often lead to diagnostic confusion. However, it is increasingly recognized in the setting of widespread anticoagulant use and aging populations.</p>
        <p>The pathogenesis involves rupture of the epigastric vessels or rectus muscle fibers within the rectus sheath. More than 70% of individuals diagnosed with rectus sheath hematoma are anticoagulated and concurrently experience trauma or significant force to the area. Additional risk factors include obesity, pregnancy, hypertension, and renal failure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28278.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28278.r2">[2]</xref> The clinical&#x000a0;presentation ranges from a small, self-limited hematoma to a life-threatening hemorrhage resulting in shock or abdominal compartment syndrome. Individuals&#x000a0;with rectus sheath hematoma experience a relatively sudden onset of abdominal pain, often accompanied by nausea and vomiting.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28278.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;Diagnosis can be made on physical examination, but may also require imaging, such as ultrasound or computed tomography (CT), to confirm the diagnosis and determine size.&#x000a0;A key examination finding is an abdominal mass that does not cross the midline.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28278.r3">[3]</xref> Most cases can be managed conservatively, including rest, and, when indicated, reversal of anticoagulation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28278.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28278.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>When bleeding and hemodynamic instability persist, percutaneous embolization provides effective control, and only rarely is open surgery required.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28278.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;Successful management of rectus sheath hematoma depends on early diagnosis and interprofessional&#x000a0;collaboration, including&#x000a0;emergency care, timely radiological assessment, medical management of coagulopathy and comorbidities,&#x000a0;with surgical oversight. Through coordinated care, heightened clinical awareness, and a multidisciplinary approach, morbidity and mortality associated with rectus sheath hematoma remain low.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28278.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>Rectus sheath hematoma results from injury to an epigastric artery or its perforating branches within the rectus muscle. The rectus abdominis receives its blood supply from the superior and inferior epigastric arteries. The superior epigastric artery arises from the internal thoracic artery and courses caudally within the rectus sheath to anastomose with the inferior epigastric artery. The inferior epigastric artery, a branch of the external iliac artery, travels cephalad along the posterior surface of the rectus muscle and is particularly vulnerable below the arcuate line, where it lacks a posterior rectus sheath.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28278.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>Common causes of injury to the epigastric vessels include direct trauma to the abdomen or forcible contractions during activities such as exercise or coughing, particularly in individuals who are anticoagulated. Any intervention that traverses the abdominal wall, such as laparoscopy, open surgery, or paracentesis, can cause a rectus sheath hematoma. Individuals&#x000a0;with comorbidities, such as hypertension, leukemia, and atherosclerosis, and pregnant persons have an increased risk of rectus sheath hematoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28278.r8">[8]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28278.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Rectus sheath hematoma accounts for approximately 1% to 2% of all causes of acute abdominal pain.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28278.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;In a 2016 review by Sheth et al, women were more likely than men to develop a rectus sheath hematoma, with&#x000a0;a ratio of 1.7:1, consistent with findings from other epidemiologic studies. The mean age of affected patients in the study was&#x000a0;67 years.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28278.r10">[10]</xref> The most common anatomic location for a rectus sheath hematoma is below the arcuate line.&#x000a0;Recent data reveal an overall mortality rate of less than 2%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28278.r10">[10]</xref></p>
        <p>Over&#x000a0;70% of patients presenting with rectus sheath hematoma are on anticoagulation, yet the incidence of rectus sheath hematoma&#x000a0;is not correlated with the prevalence of anticoagulation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28278.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0; Approximately 60% of patients diagnosed with rectus sheath hematoma&#x000a0;have chronic kidney disease stage&#x000a0;3 or greater. Other risk factors include abdominal wall injections, steroid or immunosuppressive therapy, forceful coughing, femoral puncture, and antiplatelet therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28278.r1">[1]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28278.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>Rupture of the superior or inferior epigastric artery or damage to rectus muscle fibers may occur following direct abdominal trauma or forceful contraction of the abdominal wall, leading to blood accumulation within the rectus abdominis muscle sheath. The clinical presentation of the resulting hematoma varies depending on whether it arises in the upper or lower abdomen. Injury to the superior epigastric artery typically results in a smaller hematoma that is rapidly tamponaded within the rectus sheath. In contrast, rupture of the inferior epigastric artery is less readily contained due to the absence of a posterior rectus sheath below the arcuate line, allowing for the development of larger and more clinically significant hematomas that can readily expand into the preperitoneal space.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28278.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>Rectus sheath hematomas are classified into 3 types. Type I is unilateral and confined within the rectus muscle.&#x000a0;Type II, which may be unilateral or bilateral, extends between the muscle and the transversalis fascia. Type III,&#x000a0;typically presenting with a palpable mass and significant pain, extends into the peritoneum or preperitoneal space.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28278.r11">[11]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28278.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>In a large case series of 126 patients with rectus sheath hematoma, Cherry et al reported that the most common presenting symptoms were abdominal pain (84%) and a palpable abdominal wall mass (63%).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28278.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;The pain is often located in the lower abdomen, acute in onset, sharp, severe, persistent, and exacerbated by movement. A&#x000a0;recent history of severe coughing or sneezing, or direct trauma, may be present. The patient may report associated nausea and&#x000a0;vomiting and, in cases of more significant bleeding, lightheadedness.</p>
        <p>Physical examination may reveal a unilateral or bilateral firm, nonpulsatile, tender abdominal mass. Pain increases with abdominal wall contraction (Carnett sign), and the mass remains palpable&#x000a0; (Fothergill sign), suggesting an abdominal wall source rather than an intra-abdominal etiology.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28278.r7">[7]</xref>&#x000a0;Lower abdominal ecchymosis may be visible, and the abdomen may be frankly distended. Peritonitis may be present in approximately 9.5% of cases, particularly when the hematoma extends posteriorly below the arcuate line. A sizeable rectus sheath hematoma&#x000a0;can produce hemodynamic changes, presenting as tachycardia, hypotension, and syncope, but patients typically do not exhibit signs of hemorrhagic shock until 15% to 30% of blood volume is lost, which occurs in only 1% to 13% of cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28278.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28278.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>Imaging and laboratory studies are key to evaluating rectus sheath hematoma. Initial and serial hemoglobin and hematocrit values are important for gauging the severity and clinical course of the hematoma. A coagulation panel is important, whether the patient is on anticoagulation or to establish a baseline.&#x000a0;Approximately 55% of patients presenting with rectus sheath hematoma demonstrate a decrease in hemoglobin of at least 0.4 g/dL from baseline.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28278.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;For patients taking warfarin, the international normalized ratio can guide reversal strategies, if indicated. Although the international normalized ratio is less informative in patients on antiplatelet agents or non-vitamin K anticoagulants, it should still be included as part of a comprehensive coagulation panel. If the patient is taking non-vitamin K antagonists or antiplatelet agents, evaluation includes identifying the dosing schedule, estimating bleeding severity, and assessing renal function. The&#x000a0;HAS-BLED Score&#x000a0;is one risk assessment tool for those on anticoagulation that factors in hypertension, history of stroke, and alcohol use. Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/161524">HAS-BLED Score</ext-link>,"&#x000a0;for further information. If indicated, specific laboratory tests are available for direct thrombin inhibitors and factor Xa inhibitors. In contrast, patients receiving antiplatelet therapy are assessed clinically in the absence of a specific laboratory test.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28278.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>Imaging for a clinically suspicious rectus sheath hematoma is important to characterize the location and size. Ultrasonography is a&#x000a0;quick, noninvasive&#x000a0;imaging modality, with a reported sensitivity and specificity of approximately 80%. Ultrasound can be used for rapid serial measurements and is preferred in pregnant persons and those with kidney disease, but Doppler is unreliable for detecting active extravasation. Ultrasound accuracy is operator-dependent and may be limited by body habitus and patient tolerance to<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>pressure on the abdominal wall. Ultrasound findings include a hypoechoic or heterogeneous fluid collection within the rectus muscle. CT is the gold standard, with nearly 100% sensitivity and specificity. Typical findings include a spindle-shaped mass of varying density located posterior to the rectus abdominis muscle, often accompanied by surrounding soft-tissue edema. Contrast-enhanced CT can identify active bleeding, observed as a hyperdense contrast blush.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28278.r7">[7]</xref> Magnetic resonance imaging may be considered when CT is contraindicated, and ultrasonography is inconclusive.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28278.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Most cases of rectus sheath hematoma resolve without the need for invasive intervention, as supported by multiple case series. Supportive measures&#x000a0;include rest, intravenous fluid resuscitation, ice, compression, and analgesia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28278.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;For patients with pharmacologic coagulopathy, cessation or reversal of anticoagulants is often sufficient to&#x000a0;mitigate bleeding. Patients with symptomatic anemia may benefit from transfusion of blood products, and those exhibiting hemodynamic instability may require intervention, usually in the form of angioembolization.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28278.r14">[14]</xref> Surgical intervention is rarely necessary and, in many cases, laparotomy carries a higher risk of exacerbating bleeding, particularly in patients with underlying coagulopathy.</p>
        <p>Treatment varies depending on the hematoma size and the patient's comorbidities. Type I hematomas resorb spontaneously within 30 days, and often these patients&#x000a0;can be managed as outpatients. Type II hematomas require close monitoring, bed rest, intravenous fluid replacement, and analgesia. Type III hematomas may require blood product transfusions and reversal of anticoagulation, using vitamin K, fresh frozen plasma, or specific reversal agents. Following stabilization or resolution of the hematoma,&#x000a0;the need for anticoagulation is weighed against the risk of rebleeding.&#x000a0;Patients at high risk for a thromboembolic event restart anticoagulation as soon as clinically feasible.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28278.r7">[7]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28278.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>Rectus sheath hematoma often presents as acute abdominal pain, with a wide differential diagnosis. Depending on the location and characteristics of the patient's pain, the differential may include appendicitis, diverticulitis, incarcerated hernia, biliary colic, urinary tract infection, intra-abdominal malignancy,&#x000a0;ovarian cyst, ovarian&#x000a0;torsion, or endometriosis. Additional possible diagnoses include small or large bowel obstruction; perforated viscus; vascular pathology, such as abdominal aortic aneurysm; tumors of the abdominal wall; and rectus nerve entrapment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28278.r15">[15]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28278.s10" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>Most patients recover without complications. Type I hematomas resolve within 1 month, and larger hematomas over 2 or 3 months.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28278.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;Complications are rare and include infection and abdominal compartment syndrome. A rectus sheath hematoma is fatal in approximately 2% to 5% of cases, with a worse prognosis in older patients on anticoagulant therapy. The recurrence rate is higher&#x000a0;in patients&#x000a0;receiving anticoagulation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28278.r17">[17]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28278.s11" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Although most rectus sheath hematomas resolve with conservative management, a delay in diagnosis, especially in the setting of a larger hematoma in an anticoagulated person, can lead to significant blood loss that can cause acute renal failure, myocardial infarction, necrosis of the rectus abdominis, or hemorrhagic shock. Rarely, the hematoma may become infected, requiring antibiotics and drainage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28278.r18">[18]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28278.s12" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Prompt diagnosis and patient education, particularly in vulnerable populations, are critical to improving outcomes associated with rectus sheath hematoma.&#x000a0;A delay in diagnosis can lead to preventable complications or unnecessary interventions. Patients receiving anticoagulant or antiplatelet therapy should be educated about the risk of bleeding, the importance of adherence to prescribed dosing, and the need for recommended monitoring. Clinicians should counsel patients to promptly report warning symptoms such as new-onset abdominal pain, abdominal wall swelling, or visible bruising, as early evaluation may prevent severe hemorrhagic complications. Patients should also be advised to seek medical attention if symptoms develop in the setting of increased intra-abdominal strain, such as persistent coughing or strenuous activity.</p>
        <p>In hospitalized or chronically anticoagulated patients, careful medication reconciliation is essential to avoid drug&#x02013;drug interactions that may increase bleeding risk. Preventive strategies should include fall risk assessment in older adults and appropriate dose adjustments in patients with renal impairment. Patients should be counseled on the risks and benefits of resuming anticoagulation following a rectus sheath hematoma. Overall, a combination of patient education, clinician vigilance, and coordinated follow-up can reduce preventable cases and support early detection and management of complications.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28278.s13" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Rectus sheath hematoma is an uncommon but clinically significant cause of acute abdominal pain, most often associated with abdominal wall trauma or anticoagulation, with a variable presentation that may mimic other intra-abdominal pathology, resulting in delayed diagnosis. Severity ranges from self-limited bleeding to life-threatening hemorrhage. Although most cases are managed conservatively with rest, analgesia, correction of coagulopathy, and close monitoring, some large hematomas causing hemodynamic instability require timely intervention using percutaneous angioembolization.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28278.r19">[19]</xref></p>
        <p>Prognosis is generally excellent with&#x000a0;timely recognition and management, but it depends on collaborative interprofessional communication. Emergency and primary care clinicians play a central role in early recognition, risk stratification, and care coordination. An interdisciplinary team involves radiologists, internists, pharmacists, surgeons, nurses, and ancillary staff.&#x000a0;Patients on chronic anticoagulation require careful handling of risk mitigation. During trials of conservative management, the floor team plays an essential role in close monitoring and patient assessment. Effective communication, shared decision-making, and appropriate follow-up are essential to ensure safe anticoagulation management, prevent recurrence, and optimize patient-centered outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28278.r20">[20]</xref></p>
      </sec>
